## config surface of the pinned litestream, measured

endpoint and region are accepted as replica URL query parameters and are
honoured: the log line then reads type=s3 region=<r> endpoint=https://<e>.

Unknown replica keys are SILENTLY IGNORED. A config carrying
`sse-customer-key` replicated with no error and no encryption, which means
an encryption setting that this version does not implement would read as
working. Decision 3 (encryption) must therefore be settled at the bucket or
by an explicitly verified mechanism, never by writing a key into the config
and observing that litestream starts.

The same applies to `force-path-style` as a query parameter: accepted
silently, no effect observed. §6 gate 3 must record the minimal WORKING
config, which is the only way to tell a working setting from an ignored one.

`litestream restore` can EXIT 0 AND PRODUCE A DATABASE WITH NONE OF THE
SOURCE TABLES. Restoring from a bare replica directory ("file://<dir>")
succeeded and wrote a 16 KiB file whose tables were all absent. A gate that
checked only the exit code would record that as a passing restore. Every
restore in §6 gate 5 must compare rows, not statuses.

`litestream status` and `litestream ltx` require the database to be present in
the CONFIG FILE, so neither can probe a database that was added with
`litestream register`. For a database added at runtime the daemon log is the
only signal available.

A REPLICA ROOT IS NOT NAMESPACED PER DATABASE. Two databases sharing one
replica path interleave their LTX segments, and `litestream restore` for one
then returns the OTHER database snapshot: exit status 0, wrong file. Every
S3 config must therefore give each database its own prefix, e.g.
s3://<bucket>/<db-name>. The gateway holds N databases, so this is not
optional bookkeeping; it is what makes gate 5 restore the right database.

A config file is read at STARTUP. Adding a database to the file while the
daemon runs does nothing; `litestream start` then answers "database not
found". Adding one at runtime requires `litestream register -socket <sock>
